<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The release version: one number for the whole build, semantic (1.0.0 at the public release), set here and moved by the
    release batches. Every assembly carries it; the programs' version lines read it at run time from the assembly's
    informational version (Vitomir.Platform's ReleaseVersion), so no source file holds a version of its own.

    A development build may carry a development tag beside the number: a props file beside src/, when
    there is one, sets VitomirBatch, and Vitomir.Platform alone records it, in a file beside its assembly
    (vitomir-batch.txt), so the version line reads "Vitomir Platform 1.0.0-rc.1 (tag)". Without the file the line is the release version alone.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>1.0.0-rc.1</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    The notice after the version line where a player sees it (the game's main menu, the launcher's footer and About):
    empty here, so a developer's build shows none; tools/package.ps1 sets it (-Notice, "Private beta - please do not
    distribute" until public 1.0). Recorded like the tag, in a file beside Vitomir.Platform (vitomir-notice.txt).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VitomirNotice Condition="'$(VitomirNotice)' == ''"></VitomirNotice>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The same code builds the same assemblies, wherever it is built: a network game's join compares what the
    engine's, the classic game's, the scripts' and the formats' assemblies' code is (Engine.Net.NetIdentity.CodeOf: their
    metadata and IL, not their module versions), so a friend's Release build of the public copy and a Release build of this
    tree of the same code agree. The build is
    deterministic (above); the source revision is not added to the informational version (no "+commit": the packaging script's
    build-info.json and the log's first line carry the commit instead); and in Release the source folder is mapped to /_/,
    so the folder a tree is built in is not compiled in. Debug keeps the real paths, for the debugger. The development tag
    (VitomirBatch, above) is a file beside Vitomir.Platform, not compiled into it: a portable PDB records the module version
    of every assembly a project is compiled against, so a tag in Vitomir.Platform would reach the four through it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>false</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
    <PathMap Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">$([MSBuild]::EnsureTrailingSlash($([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..'))))=/_/</PathMap>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\build\batch.props" Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\build\batch.props')" />

  <!--
    A warning fails the build. Every warning the C# compiler or an analyzer gives (nullable warnings
    and the .NET code-analysis rules below among them) is an error, in every project, so a warning cannot be left to pile up
    unread. `dotnet build Vitomir.slnx` lists them, each with its rule id and its file and line; a build that compiles nothing
    new lists none, so a rebuild (the build command with the no-incremental switch) shows them all again.

    The analyzers are the .NET SDK's own (Microsoft's code-analysis rules, CAxxxx) in the SDK's default mode. AnalysisLevel is
    pinned to 10.0, not "latest", so a newer SDK on a Windows PC or in the cloud cannot turn new rules on and fail the build by
    itself; raising it is a change of its own. Code style (IDExxxx) is `.editorconfig`'s business, not the build's, so
    EnforceCodeStyleInBuild stays off.

    MSBuild's own warnings (a <Warning> task's, such as the renderers' "dxc is not on PATH" note in the cloud, which has no
    dxc) are not compiler warnings and stay warnings: MSBuildTreatWarningsAsErrors is deliberately not set.

    NuGet takes TreatWarningsAsErrors too. Its audit warnings (NU1901 to NU1904: a package we use has a published security
    advisory, low to critical) stay warnings: an advisory is published about a package, not made by a change here, so as an
    error it would fail a build of an unchanged tree on the day it appeared (as a new SDK's rules would, above). It is
    still shown on every restore, and answered by a change of its own (moving the package). NuGet's other warnings fail.

    An exception is made where it is needed and nowhere else, with its reason written beside it: a `#pragma warning disable
    CAxxxx // why` around the lines, or the rule set off for a folder or for generated code in `.editorconfig` with a comment
    (the README's "Build, test and run").
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevel>10.0</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>false</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Build for the machine doing the building, and for no other.

    Without a runtime identifier the SDK copies every platform's native assets into every project's
    output, because a portable build has to be able to run anywhere. For this tree that is SDL3 for
    thirteen platforms — four Android ABIs and an iOS xcframework among them — and IKVM's 64 MB Java
    class library three times over, once each for Windows, Linux and macOS. It came to roughly 280 MB
    per project and about 3 GB across the tree.

    `UseCurrentRuntimeIdentifier` is the SDK's own switch for "whatever this machine is", so it
    picks `win-x64` on a Windows PC and `linux-x64` in the cloud without either being written
    down, and an arm64 machine would get the right answer too. It has to be the SDK's switch rather
    than our own copy of `NETCoreSdkRuntimeIdentifier`: this file is imported before the SDK sets
    that property, so reading it here gives an empty string and nothing happens at all.

    `SelfContained` is pinned off because a runtime identifier has implied it in some SDK versions
    and we want a framework-dependent build either way. `AppendRuntimeIdentifierToOutputPath` is off
    so the output stays at `bin/<config>/net10.0/` — there is only ever one runtime in it now, so
    the extra folder would buy nothing and every path in the docs would have to change.

    `BuildOutputTests` asserts that only one platform's natives reach the output.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseCurrentRuntimeIdentifier Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == ''">true</UseCurrentRuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>false</SelfContained>
    <AppendRuntimeIdentifierToOutputPath>false</AppendRuntimeIdentifierToOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The plain test run leaves out the timing tests (the README's "Build, test and run"). A test that holds a
    wall-clock figure to a bound is marked [Trait("Category", "Timing")]; `dotnet test Vitomir.slnx` runs all ten test
    assemblies at once, and under that load the figure measures the others' work too (HitVolumeTests once measured 21.76 µs
    against its 20 µs bound so, 1.98 µs alone). They run by a second command of their own, one assembly after another and one
    test after another (`-m:1` and Timing.runsettings).

    This is the filter `dotnet test` takes when it is given none: the command line's filter arrives as the global property
    VSTestTestCaseFilter, which this condition then leaves alone, so a FullyQualifiedName~ line runs what it names exactly as
    before, a timing test among them. (A runsettings file's TestCaseFilter was tried first and rejected: VSTest applies it
    on top of the command line's, so a named timing test would not run.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VSTestTestCaseFilter Condition="'$(VSTestTestCaseFilter)' == ''">Category!=Timing</VSTestTestCaseFilter>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
